<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 552 or 553</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teia was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, elected after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a>'s death at Taginae in 552. He gathered the remaining Gothic forces and marched south to protect the Gothic treasure at Cumae, but was trapped near Vesuvius by the Byzantine army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-lactarius">Battle of Mons Lactarius</a>, after which organised Gothic resistance collapsed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teia.jpg" alt="Teia" width="144" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regno_dei_goti,_teia,_emissione_bronzea,_552-553.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Teia's early life except that he was the elder brother of Aligernus. He served as a military officer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a>, who revived Gothic resistance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and within a decade recovered much of the Italian peninsula. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> mentions Teia in the later stages of the war, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> sent his chamberlain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> with an army to end it. Totila sent Teia with 2,000 cavalry to guard the River Po, but Narses crossed the river without fighting and marched towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Totila recalled Teia, whose reinforcements arrived just as the armies met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taginae">Battle of Taginae</a> in July 552, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> were defeated and Totila was mortally wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Teia gathered the remaining Gothic forces at Ticinum (Pavia), where they chose him king, and took possession of the treasury there. He urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> to move against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, but they preferred to let the two sides wear each other down. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with little resistance, Teia ordered the killing of all the Roman senators and their families held as hostages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, and of 300 children of prominent Italian families whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> had collected.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> then besieged Cumae, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a>'s war chest was held and Teia's brother commanded the garrison. Teia marched south, making lengthy detours that took nearly thirty days, but Narses intercepted him near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> camped near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarno-river">Sarno River</a>, and a two-month stalemate followed. It ended when the Gothic admiral defected, cutting off supplies, and Teia withdrew to Mount Lactarius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> attacked there; the sources disagree on whether the date was 1 or 30 October. Both armies fought on foot, and Teia led repeated assaults until a spear killed him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> displayed his head on a spear, yet the Goths fought on for two more days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> wrote that the battle showed a heroism inferior to that of none of the heroes of legend, and the survivors negotiated a withdrawal, on terms that differ between Procopius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathias">Agathias</a>. By 554 the Ostrogothic kingdom had faded, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins minted in Teia's name had circulated from Pavia along the Alpine trade routes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Totila was killed at Busta Gallorum in 552, and <b>Teia</b> at Mons Lactarius in 552 or 553.</blockquote><a href="/state/ostrogothic-kingdom">Ostrogothic Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Nuceria, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.74,14.67&amp;year=543" aria-label="Nuceria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4910 709 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Teia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5360 979h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5360 979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.74,14.67&amp;year=543">What was here in 543 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
